<h1>Stainless Steel Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A stainless steel conveyor uses corrosion-resistant stainless steel alloys for its frame, side rails, legs, guards, and hardware instead of painted carbon steel. The two dominant alloys are 304 stainless (general-purpose food and pharmaceutical) and 316 stainless (chloride-resistant for seafood, chemical, and coastal environments). Stainless steel is the baseline material for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food processing</a>, pharmaceutical manufacturing, and chemical handling conveyors where corrosion, contamination, and cleaning compliance drive equipment specification. The material alone does not make a conveyor sanitary or compliant: frame design, welds, finish, and documentation do.</p>
<h2 id="alloy-heading">304 vs 316 Stainless Steel</h2>
<h3>304 Stainless Steel (18-8)</h3>
<p>304 stainless steel contains 18-20% chromium and 8-10.5% nickel, per the <a href="https://www.ssina.com/education/technical-resources/composition-properties/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SSINA composition table</a>. It is the most widely used stainless steel grade in the food and beverage industry. 304 resists corrosion from water, steam, mild acids (fruit juices, vinegar), and most cleaning chemicals (alkaline detergents, quaternary sanitizers). It handles continuous washdown environments with standard CIP chemicals. 304 is the default specification for food, dairy, beverage, pharmaceutical, and personal care conveyor applications unless the environment contains chlorides or strong acids.</p>
<h3>316 Stainless Steel</h3>
<p>316 stainless steel contains 2-3% molybdenum, with slightly lower chromium (16-18%) and higher nickel (10-14%) than 304. <a href="https://www.ssina.com/education/corrosion/pitting-and-crevice-corrosion/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SSINA identifies molybdenum</a>, with chromium and nitrogen, as a primary contributor to pitting and crevice corrosion resistance, and chlorides as the usual cause of that corrosion. 316 is the usual choice in environments with salt, brine, chlorinated water, or acid exposure. Specific applications: seafood processing (saltwater and brine contact), coastal manufacturing (salt air), pharmaceutical manufacturing (acid and solvent exposure), chemical processing (chloride-bearing chemicals), and outdoor installations in marine environments. 316 costs more than 304 because of its molybdenum and higher nickel content.</p>
<h3>316L (Low Carbon)</h3>
<p>316L is a low-carbon variant of 316 (0.03% maximum carbon versus 0.08%) that resists sensitization (grain boundary carbide precipitation) during welding. <a href="https://www.ssina.com/education/corrosion/intergranular-corrosion/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SSINA notes</a> that the L grades are designed to resist sensitization during typical welding, but not during long-term service exposure in the sensitizing temperature range. Sensitization weakens the corrosion resistance of the weld heat-affected zone. 316L is specified whenever extensive welding is required, which includes most conveyor fabrication. In practice, many manufacturers use 316L as their standard 316 offering. The mechanical properties and corrosion resistance are virtually identical to 316 for conveyor applications.</p>
<h3>Other Alloys</h3>
<p>Duplex stainless steels (2205, 2507) offer higher strength and corrosion resistance than 316 for extreme chemical environments. AL-6XN (super-austenitic) handles concentrated chloride solutions. These specialty alloys cost significantly more and are used only in severe chemical processing applications, not standard food or pharmaceutical conveying. For high-temperature applications, see our <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">wire mesh belt guide</a> for Inconel and Nichrome alloy details.</p>
